      "knowledge_point": "Identifying Possible Roots of a Polynomial Using the Rational Roots Theorem",
      "problem": "According to the rational roots theorem, which of the following could be a root of the polynomial $f(x)=7{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+x-14?$",
      "worked_solution": "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $-14,$ and the leading coefficient is $7.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }-14}{\\text{factor of }7}.\n$$\n\nOf the given answer choices, only $-2=\\frac{-2}{1}$ takes the above form since the numerator $(-2)$ is a factor of $-14$ and the denominator $(1)$ is a factor of $7.$",
